## Solution

**Setting up the equation.** A segment consisting of $k$ sevens has value $7 \cdot \frac{10^k - 1}{9} = 7R_k$, where $R_k$ is the repunit $\underbrace{11\cdots1}_{k}$. If $a_k$ denotes the number of segments of length $k$, the signal-strength condition is:

$$\sum_k a_k \cdot 7R_k = 7000 \quad\Longrightarrow\quad \sum_k a_k \cdot R_k = 1000.$$

Since $R_4 = 1111 > 1000$ and all $a_k \geq 0$, only $R_1=1,\; R_2=11,\; R_3=111$ can appear. So we need non-negative integers $a_1, a_2, a_3$ with:

$$a_1 + 11a_2 + 111a_3 = 1000, \qquad n = a_1 + 2a_2 + 3a_3.$$

**Expressing $n$.** Fixing $a_3$, the remainder $r = 1000 - 111a_3$ must satisfy $a_1 + 11a_2 = r$, so $a_2$ ranges from $0$ to $M_{a_3} = \lfloor r/11\rfloor$, and $a_1 = r - 11a_2 \geq 0$ automatically. Then:

$$n = (r - 11a_2) + 2a_2 + 3a_3 = 1000 - 108a_3 - 9a_2.$$

Since $1000 \equiv 1\pmod{9}$ and $108, 9$ are multiples of $9$, every achievable $n$ satisfies $n \equiv 1 \pmod{9}$.

**Range of $a_3$:** $0 \leq a_3 \leq 9$ (since $111 \cdot 9 = 999 \leq 1000 < 1110$).

For each $a_3$, $n$ ranges from $U_k = 1000 - 108k$ (when $a_2=0$) down to $L_k = 1000 - 108k - 9M_k$ (when $a_2 = M_k$), in steps of $9$:

| $a_3$ | $M_{a_3}$ | $n$-range |
|---|---|---|
| 0 | 90 | [190, 1000] |
| 1 | 80 | [172, 892] |
| 2 | 70 | [154, 784] |
| 3 | 60 | [136, 676] |
| 4 | 50 | [118, 568] |
| 5 | 40 | [100, 460] |
| 6 | 30 | [82, 352] |
| 7 | 20 | [64, 244] |
| 8 | 10 | [46, 136] |
| 9 | 0 | {28} |

**Checking for gaps.** Consecutive ranges $a_3 = k$ and $a_3 = k+1$ connect without gaps iff $U_{k+1} \geq L_k - 9$, i.e., $M_k \geq 11$. For $k = 0, 1, \ldots, 7$, we have $M_k \in \{90, 80, 70, 60, 50, 40, 30, 20\}$, all $\geq 11$, and in fact each pair **overlaps**. So $a_3 = 0, \ldots, 8$ together form the complete arithmetic progression $\{46, 55, 64, \ldots, 1000\}$ (step $9$).

For $k = 8$: $M_8 = 10 < 11$, so there is a gap. $L_8 = 46$ and $U_9 = 28$; the value $37 = 46 - 9$ is **not** covered. Indeed, $n = 37$ requires $12a_3 + a_2 = 107$; for $a_3 = 8$ this gives $a_2 = 11 > M_8 = 10$, and for $a_3 = 9$ it gives $a_2 = -1 < 0$. So $37$ is impossible. No other gaps exist (the value $28$ is covered by $a_3 = 9$, and nothing below $28$ is achievable since $a_3 = 9$ already maximizes value-per-digit).

**Counting.** The achievable values of $n$ are:

$$\{28\} \cup \{46, 55, 64, \ldots, 1000\}.$$

The arithmetic progression $\{46, 55, \ldots, 1000\}$ has $\frac{1000 - 46}{9} + 1 = \frac{954}{9} + 1 = 107$ terms. Adding the isolated value $28$:

$$107 + 1 = \boxed{108}.$$

